From: perf session 2026-09-11
Subject: [PATCH 0019] wasm64: speculative successor translation, compile-once batching, compaction (Firefox OOM fix)

Backend workstream, the early-boot deficit (playbook § Remaining #1) and
the Firefox out-of-memory report.  /dist-jit only (every hunk outside
tcg/wasm64/ and ui/wasm.c is under CONFIG_TCG_WASM64).

The backend compiled one throwaway WebAssembly.Module per TB at first
execution plus a batch module per 128 TBs: ~1-2k modules/s in the early
boot, 21 % of vCPU self-time in `Module` (wprof2 PROF_DELAY=8), and in
Firefox the process-wide executable budget (~16.3k live modules, 64 KB
page each; dropped modules are not GC pressure and a never-yielding
worker never collects them) ran out in 10 s.

1. Speculative successor translation (cpu-exec.c w64_speculate):
   translator_use_goto_tb records each TB's goto_tb destinations; on a
   lookup miss the successors are translated breadth-first (through
   already-translated TBs, W64_SPEC_N default 16) into the open batch.
   Guards: plain cflags only; NON-FAULTING probes (probe_access_full_mmu)
   of the target and the following page BEFORE tb_htable_lookup - the
   faulting get_page_addr_code delivered a prefetch abort to the guest
   for a blx Thumb target recorded in ARM mode (alignment fault) and
   would for any unmapped never-taken target; 1 MB code-buffer headroom
   (no flush from here; a flush is detected and the caller re-looks-up).
2. Compile once, at first execution (tcg_qemu_tb_exec): a TB pending in
   the open batch closes the batch (miss TB + speculated successors, one
   module) instead of a temp module; temp path kept as fallback
   (W64_NOCLOSEEXEC=1 for A/B).
3. Landed-batch records (member/fixup/union tables; bodies stay staged
   in the code buffer) + live FIFO cap (W64_LIVE_MAX, 6144) + on-demand
   re-assembly of an evicted batch (w64_batch_ensure; the goto_tb fidx
   brake makes evicted chains fall through).
4. Compaction (w64_compact): every 256 live small batches / 1024 members
   merge into one module (union tables rebuilt, 2-byte call-fixup LEBs
   remapped, bodies compiled once more at bulk rate - per-module fixed
   cost dominates: 20k x 2 KB modules 553 ms vs 312 x 129 KB 75 ms in
   V8) and the small modules are dropped: 162 live modules after 25 s
   (6144 at the cap alone, 7168 re-ensures -> 0).
5. GC nudge in w64_batch_instantiate: a throwaway 32 MB ArrayBuffer every
   256 instantiations keeps a Firefox worker collecting dropped modules
   (measured: 6000-module FIFO OOMs at 16.3k created without it).
6. Diagnostics exports (ui/wasm.c): wasm_pc, wasm_reg, wasm_irq_pending,
   wasm_peek; W64_TBLOG=1, W64_DEBUG=1 speculation/compaction counters.

Measured (idlebench --runs 2, interleaved, quiet host; both candidate
runs beat both baseline runs on every metric):
  tIdle     76.2 / 71.5 s  ->  65.0 / 64.9 s   (-15 %)
  window    30.4 / 28.7    ->  26.0 / 25.7     (-14 %)
  t0.25G    24.8 -> 21.7 (-13 %)   t0.5G 48.7 -> 41.7 (-14 %)
  t0.75G    68.4 -> 58.1 (-15 %)   t1.3G 74.1 -> 63.3 (-15 %)
  rss       1924-2110 MB -> 1848-1850 MB
  Firefox 155 (Playwright): boots to the idle screen, no console errors
  (was: out of memory at 10 s).
Gates: op-suite 1156/1156 (native JIT/TCI/wasm TCI/wasm64 byte-identical),
lockstep 20e6 + 250e6 clean (one-insn-per-tb), native suite 4/4 PASS.
Sessions doc 2026-09-11 (0019) has the forensics of the alignment-fault
divergence and the Firefox module-budget probes.

